{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB14568 - MONDO_17893",
  "verbalization": "Ivosidenib is associated with Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia through a chain of indications that link it to Mitoxantrone, which in turn is linked to the disease. The evidence suggests that Ivosidenib may be repurposed for use in cases of Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia, particularly when the condition is linked to Mitoxantrone. This relationship is supported by the shared indication of Mitoxantrone with Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia, indicating a potential mechanistic or therapeutic connection. The presence of multiple pathways involving Mitoxantrone and Ivosidenib suggests that the drug may have a role in treating this form of leukemia, possibly through a mechanism involving the inhibition of topoisomerase II, which is also implicated in the pathogenesis of the disease. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Ivosidenib to be a candidate for repurposing in the treatment of Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia.",
